<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In-context reinforcement learning (ICRL) studies agents that, after pretraining, adapt to new tasks by conditioning on additional context without parameter updates.
Existing theoretical analyses of ICRL largely rely on linear attention,
which replaces the softmax function in the standard attention with an identity mapping.
This paper provides the first theoretical understanding of ICRL without making the unrealistic linear attention simplification.
In particular, we consider the standard
softmax attention used in practice.
We show that, with certain parameters, the layerwise forward pass of a Transformer with such softmax attention is equivalent to iterative updates of a weighted softmax temporal difference (TD) learning algorithm.
Here, weighted softmax TD is a new RL algorithm that performs policy evaluation in kernel space and adopts both linear TD and tabular TD as special cases.
We also prove that under a certain contraction condition,
the policy evaluation error decays as the number of layers grows,
with the identified parameters above.
Finally, we prove that those parameters are a global minimizer of a pretraining loss,
explaining their emergence in our numerical experiments.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works on in-context reinforcement learning (ICRL, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moeini et al. (2025)</a></cite>) suggest that an RL agent can adapt to a new task at inference time without updating its parameters.
In this framework, an agent network is pretrained on a distribution of tasks and then deployed with fixed pretrained parameters.
At test time, the agent conditions on additional context generated within the new task, such as an interaction history <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}\doteq(S_{0},A_{0},R_{1},\ldots,S_{t-1},A_{t-1},R_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}\doteq(S_{0},A_{0},R_{1},\ldots,S_{t-1},A_{t-1},R_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and outputs actions or value estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laskin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Such fixed-parameter adaptation has been observed in
algorithm distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laskin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, human-timescale adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bauer et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, long-context embodied agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elawady et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Transformer-based adaptive agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raparthy et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grigsby et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grigsby et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and continuous-control or locomotion agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beaussant and Mounsif, 2025</a>)</cite>.
Since the pretrained parameters do not change at test time, any adjustment in actions or value estimates must be produced by the
forward pass using the new context.
This motivates the question we study: what RL process is implemented by the fixed agent network when it uses the context for adaptation?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We study this question in the reinforcement-pretraining regime.
This regime differs from supervised pretraining, where the desired
update rule is already present in the training targets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laskin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zisman et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirsch et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
In reinforcement pretraining, the agent is instead optimized through task-level RL objectives, such as producing good actions in control or accurate value estimates in policy evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grigsby et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Thus, any context-dependent update implemented in the forward pass should be understood as an emergent computation induced by the pretraining objective, rather than as a imitated update rule.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To make this question concrete, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> and consider in-context policy evaluation.
In each task, a fixed policy <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> induces a value function <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The agent receives a query state <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and a context <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of transitions collected under <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and outputs an estimate of <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> using its fixed pretrained parameters.
The same agent network must handle a family of evaluation tasks whose transition dynamics, reward functions, and policies may differ. Temporal difference learning (TD, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton (1988)</a></cite>) is a
standard algorithm for policy evaluation, making it a natural candidate for the RL process implemented in the forward pass.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> prove that, under a carefully designed parameterization, the layer-by-layer forward pass of linear
Transformers is equivalent to step-by-step TD updates.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> further prove that those parameters can attain global optimality for a reinforcement-pretraining objective.
However, these results largely rely on linear self-attention where the softmax function in attention is replaced by an identity mapping to simplify analysis.
In this work, we remove this simplification by studying the standard
softmax attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> used in practice.
We ask whether comparable in-context policy evaluation behavior can still arise under this setting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07333v1/msve.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/143;" width="215" height="143"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>In-context policy evaluation with a 15-layer dual-head Transformer using softmax attention.
The model takes the context <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and a query state <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}(\tau_{t},s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>TF</mtext><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}(\tau_{t},s)</annotation></semantics></math> as the estimation of the state value <math id="S1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The y-axis is the mean square value error (MSVE) <math id="S1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{s}d_{\pi}(s)(\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}(\tau_{t},s)-v_{\pi}(s))^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>TF</mtext><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{s}d_{\pi}(s)(\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}(\tau_{t},s)-v_{\pi}(s))^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> being the stationary state distribution.
The curves are averaged over 300 randomly generated policy evaluation tasks, with shaded regions indicating standard errors.
Across tasks, the state space, transition dynamics, reward function, and policy vary, while a single parameterization <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is shared.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives the motivating construction.
Let <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>TF</mtext><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the dual-head Transformer with a manually constructed parameterization <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> used in Figure <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>.
Given a context <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and a query state <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the model outputs <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TF}_{\theta_{*}}(\tau_{t},s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>TF</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TF}_{\theta_{*}}(\tau_{t},s)</annotation></semantics></math> as an estimate of <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We report the value approximation error averaged over many tasks and policies with distinct value functions, using a single fixed <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout.
Notably, this error decreases as the context length <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> grows.
Since the target value functions vary widely across tasks, this improvement is unlikely to be explained by hard-coding <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to any particular true value function.
Instead, the model appears to leverage the context to refine its prediction by performing a policy-evaluation update in its forward pass.
This observation leads to three central questions.</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Q1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What specific policy-evaluation algorithm is being implemented by <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>TF</mtext><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TF}_{\theta_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Q2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What convergence guarantees hold as the number of layers grows?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Q3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What kind of pretraining could give rise to these parameters?</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the above questions by providing the first theoretical analysis of in-context policy evaluation under standard softmax attention.
We show that the empirical pattern in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be explained by a novel algorithm, which we term <span id="S1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">weighted softmax TD</span>.
We provide a constructive design of a dual-head Transformer layer whose forward pass implements a batch analogue of the nonparametric TD update <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berthier et al., 2022</a>)</cite> with softmax weighting.
Moreover, we show that this dual-head layer admits an equivalent reparameterization as single-head attention composed with a variant of the Temporal Shift Module (TSM, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2019)</a></cite>), which is parameter-free.
This reparameterization reduces computation and further simplifies our inference-time convergence analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, our contributions answer <a href="#S1.I1.i1" title="item (Q1) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q1)</span></a>-<a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item (Q3) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q3)</span></a> in turn.
For <a href="#S1.I1.i1" title="item (Q1) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q1)</span></a>, we construct a dual-head Transformer layer that implements a weighted softmax TD update step in its forward pass.
For <a href="#S1.I1.i2" title="item (Q2) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q2)</span></a>, we prove an inference-time convergence guarantee as depth grows, under a suitable contraction condition.
For <a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item (Q3) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q3)</span></a>, we show that the desired parameters are global minimizers of a pretraining loss, explaining their emergence in our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by establishing the technical foundations for our work. All vectors are treated as column vectors. We denote the identity matrix in <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0_{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0_{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote zero matrix and all-ones matrix.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the standard basis vector for the <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row.
The transpose of a matrix <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inner product of two vectors <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by either <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x,y\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x,y\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\top}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\top}y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Transformer’s core innovation is the self-attention mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
Given a prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, a standard single-head
self-attention layer processes the prompt via</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\text{Attn}_{W_{v},W_{q},W_{k}}(Z)\doteq W_{v}Z\,\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z^{\top}W_{k}^{\top}W_{q}Z+M\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\text{Attn}_{W_{v},W_{q},W_{k}}(Z)\doteq W_{v}Z\,\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z^{\top}W_{k}^{\top}W_{q}Z+M\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(d+3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(d+3)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times(d+3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times(d+3)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times(d+3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times(d+3)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the value, key, and query weight
matrices, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softmax}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied column-wise.
The mask <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> isolates the query column from the context columns</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle M[i,j]=\begin{cases}0,&amp;i\leq n,\\
-\infty,&amp;i=n+1,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle M[i,j]=\begin{cases}0,&amp;i\leq n,\\
-\infty,&amp;i=n+1,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">so that the query column does not contribute as a source token.
For analytical convenience, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. (2024)</a></cite> by reparameterizing <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and denoting the query-key score matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}^{\top}W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}^{\top}W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer Transformer with parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(V_{l},A_{l})\}_{l=0,\ldots,L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(V_{l},A_{l})\}_{l=0,\ldots,L-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
updates the representation at layer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l+1</annotation></semantics></math> recursively from an initial
prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle Z_{l+1}\doteq Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\,\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}+M\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle Z_{l+1}\doteq Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\,\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}+M\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We define the attention matrix at layer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\widetilde{K}_{l}\doteq\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}+M\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times(n+1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\widetilde{K}_{l}\doteq\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}+M\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times(n+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Its entry <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}_{l}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}_{l}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the attention weight assigned to source token <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> when aggregating into token <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}_{l}[n+1,j]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}_{l}[n+1,j]=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
For notational simplicity, we suppress <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in the remainder of the paper, with the understanding that the mask in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is applied throughout.
Since policy evaluation requires a scalar prediction, we extract a single entry from the final representation matrix.
Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, we define</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TF}_{L}\left(Z_{0};\{V_{l},A_{l}\}_{l=0}^{L-1}\right)\doteq Z_{L}[d+3,n+1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>TF</mtext><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TF}_{L}\left(Z_{0};\{V_{l},A_{l}\}_{l=0}^{L-1}\right)\doteq Z_{L}[d+3,n+1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">to denote the output of the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer Transformer given an input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison, the existing ICRL theory of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> uses linear attention, replacing the masked softmax in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) with the identity map:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle Z_{l+1}^{\mathrm{lin}}=Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\,(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>lin</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle Z_{l+1}^{\mathrm{lin}}=Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\,(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In the broader in-context learning literature, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. (2024)</a></cite> further replace the softmax with a general activation <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, giving
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\,h(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\,h(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We instead retain the standard softmax <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, bringing our analysis closer to the architecture used in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Reinforcement Learning and Policy Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since we focus on the policy evaluation problem with a fixed policy, we work directly with the induced Markov Reward Process (MRP, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bellman (1957)</a></cite>).
An MRP is a tuple <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{S},p,r,\gamma,p_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{S},p,r,\gamma,p_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of a finite state space <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, a transition kernel <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, a reward function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, a discount factor <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and an initial distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, an MRP arises from a Markov Decision Process <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},p_{\text{MDP}},r_{\text{MDP}},\gamma,p_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>MDP</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>MDP</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},p_{\text{MDP}},r_{\text{MDP}},\gamma,p_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> under a fixed policy <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> by marginalizing over actions: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(s^{\prime}|s)=\sum_{a}\pi(a|s)p_{\text{MDP}}(s^{\prime}|s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>MDP</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(s^{\prime}|s)=\sum_{a}\pi(a|s)p_{\text{MDP}}(s^{\prime}|s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(s)=\sum_{a}\pi(a|s)r_{\text{MDP}}(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>MDP</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(s)=\sum_{a}\pi(a|s)r_{\text{MDP}}(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A trajectory <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{0},R_{1},S_{1},R_{2},\dots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{0},R_{1},S_{1},R_{2},\dots)</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled by drawing
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and, at each step, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{t+1}\sim p(\cdot|S_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t+1}\sim p(\cdot|S_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t+1}\doteq r(S_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t+1}\doteq r(S_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the state transition matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times|\mathcal{S}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times|\mathcal{S}|}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}[s,s^{\prime}]=p(s^{\prime}|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}[s,s^{\prime}]=p(s^{\prime}|s)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and assume the Markov chain induced by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is ergodic on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with unique stationary distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\pi}:\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\pi}:\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We aim to estimate the value function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}(s)\doteq\mathbb{E}\quantity[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \gamma^k R_{t+k+1} \mid S_t = s]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}(s)\doteq\mathbb{E}\quantity[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \gamma^k R_{t+k+1} \mid S_t = s]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One fundamental task in RL is policy evaluation, where the goal is estimating the value function <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique fixed point of the Bellman expectation operator <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{\pi}:\mathbb{R}^{\absolutevalue{\fS}}\to\mathbb{R}^{\absolutevalue{\fS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{\pi}:\mathbb{R}^{\absolutevalue{\fS}}\to\mathbb{R}^{\absolutevalue{\fS}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T}^{\pi}v)(s)\doteq\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[R_{t+1}+\gamma v(S_{t+1})|S_{t}=s]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T}^{\pi}v)(s)\doteq\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[R_{t+1}+\gamma v(S_{t+1})|S_{t}=s].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Temporal-Difference learning (TD, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton (1988)</a></cite>) is among the most influential and widely used algorithms for policy evaluation.
For large or continuous state spaces where tabular representations are
impractical, a long line of work has combined RL with kernel methods to
obtain non-parametric value function approximation
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ormoneit and Sen, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farahmand et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koppel et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berthier et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. We follow the formulation of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berthier et al. (2022)</a></cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a measurable state
space and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> a positive-definite
kernel with associated reproducing kernel Hilbert space <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the TD error at a transition <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(x,x^{\prime})\doteq r(x)+\gamma v(x^{\prime})-v(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(x,x^{\prime})\doteq r(x)+\gamma v(x^{\prime})-v(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Given a sampled transition <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t},X_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},X_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, non-parametric TD updates the value estimate <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> at every query state <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> via</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t+1}(y)=v_{t}(y)+\alpha_{t}\,\delta(X_{t},X_{t+1})\,\kappa(X_{t},y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t+1}(y)=v_{t}(y)+\alpha_{t}\,\delta(X_{t},X_{t+1})\,\kappa(X_{t},y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of learning rates.
In this paper we work with the finite state space <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Restricting (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In 2.2 Reinforcement Learning and Policy Evaluation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) to
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and to a query state <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the update</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t+1}(S_{n})=v_{t}(S_{n})+\alpha_{t}\,\delta(S_{t},S_{t+1})\,\kappa(S_{t},S_{n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t+1}(S_{n})=v_{t}(S_{n})+\alpha_{t}\,\delta(S_{t},S_{t+1})\,\kappa(S_{t},S_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">which is the form we build on in the rest of the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now answer <a href="#S1.I1.i1" title="item (Q1) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q1)</span></a> by introducing a new RL algorithm, <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighted softmax TD</span>, and constructing a dual-head Transformer that implements its update in the forward pass. Notably, this construction reveals the parameters we use to generate the results in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Weighted softmax TD.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a trajectory <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}=(S_{0},R_{1},\dots,S_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}=(S_{0},R_{1},\dots,S_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from an MRP, weighted softmax TD forms TD errors using (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.2 Reinforcement Learning and Policy Evaluation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with the sampled reward instantiated by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}^{(t)}\doteq R_{k}+\gamma v_{t}(S_{k})-v_{t}(S_{k-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}^{(t)}\doteq R_{k}+\gamma v_{t}(S_{k})-v_{t}(S_{k-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> indexes transitions within <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> indexes the iteration.
Non-parametric TD in (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2.2 Reinforcement Learning and Policy Evaluation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) updates value estimates using a single transition.
We introduce weighted softmax TD as a batch variant that aggregates <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> through softmax weights.
Specifically, for the trajectory states <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{j}\}_{j=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{j}\}_{j=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the softmax weight assigned to the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th transition by</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle K(S_{k-1},S_{j})\doteq\frac{\exp(g(S_j,S_{k-1}))}{\sum_{m=1}^{n}\exp(g(S_j,S_{m-1}))},\quad k=1,\dots,n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle K(S_{k-1},S_{j})\doteq\frac{\exp(g(S_j,S_{k-1}))}{\sum_{m=1}^{n}\exp(g(S_j,S_{m-1}))},\quad k=1,\dots,n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a score function.
Thus, the value estimates <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{t}(S_{j})\}_{j=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{t}(S_{j})\}_{j=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle v_{t+1}(S_{j})=v_{t}(S_{j})+\alpha_{t}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\delta_{k}^{(t)}K(S_{k-1},S_{j})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle v_{t+1}(S_{j})=v_{t}(S_{j})+\alpha_{t}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\delta_{k}^{(t)}K(S_{k-1},S_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(weighted softmax TD)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, if <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(S_{j},S_{k-1})=\log\kappa(S_{j},S_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(S_{j},S_{k-1})=\log\kappa(S_{j},S_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> for some positive similarity function <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, then the weights reduce to
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(S_{k-1},S_{j})=\frac{\kappa(S_{j},S_{k-1})}{\sum_{m}\kappa(S_{j},S_{m-1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(S_{k-1},S_{j})=\frac{\kappa(S_{j},S_{k-1})}{\sum_{m}\kappa(S_{j},S_{m-1})}</annotation></semantics></math>, recovering a normalized form of (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2.2 Reinforcement Learning and Policy Evaluation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We next show that the dual-head Transformer used in generating Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implements the (<a href="#S3.Ex1" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">weighted softmax TD</span></a>) in a single forward pass.
We begin by specifying the prompt construction and the layerwise update rule.
We encode the trajectory <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into a prompt matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+3)\times(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the last column corresponds to the query state <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using shorthands <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}\doteq x(S_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}\doteq x(S_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\doteq\begin{bmatrix}x_{0}&amp;x_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-1}&amp;x_{n}\\
R_{1}&amp;R_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;R_{n}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}X\\
R\\
\mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0}\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>X</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>R</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\doteq\begin{bmatrix}x_{0}&amp;x_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;x_{n-1}&amp;x_{n}\\
R_{1}&amp;R_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;R_{n}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}X\\
R\\
\mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{0}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, the last two rows serve as memory that the network writes and reuses across layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer Transformer indexed by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=0,1,\dots,L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=0,1,\dots,L-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each layer consists of two attention heads, both taking <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as input.
To state the layer update clearly, we define <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta Z_{l}=0_{(d+3)\times(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Z_{l}=0_{(d+3)\times(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then construct two heads as follows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Current-value head.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quantity{(V_l, A_l)}_{l=0,1,\dots,L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quantity{(V_l, A_l)}_{l=0,1,\dots,L-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the parameters of the current-value head in the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer Transformer.
It writes an additive update to row <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+3</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta Z_{l}[d+3,:]=\big(V_{l}Z_{l}\,\mathrm{softmax}(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})\big)[d+3,:]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Z_{l}[d+3,:]=\big(V_{l}Z_{l}\,\mathrm{softmax}(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})\big)[d+3,:].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We choose <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{l},A_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{l},A_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle V_{l}\doteq\begin{bmatrix}0_{(d+2)\times d}&amp;0_{(d+2)\times 3}\\
0_{1\times d}&amp;\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;-1\end{bmatrix}\end{bmatrix},\,\,A_{l}\doteq\begin{bmatrix}I_{d}&amp;0_{d\times 3}\\
0_{3\times d}&amp;0_{3\times 3}\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle V_{l}\doteq\begin{bmatrix}0_{(d+2)\times d}&amp;0_{(d+2)\times 3}\\
0_{1\times d}&amp;\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;-1\end{bmatrix}\end{bmatrix},\,\,A_{l}\doteq\begin{bmatrix}I_{d}&amp;0_{d\times 3}\\
0_{3\times d}&amp;0_{3\times 3}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By construction, the only non-zero row of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is its last row, and hence (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) modifies only the last row of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With the choice of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), the score matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the first <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> rows of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, which by (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) store the fixed trajectory features <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quantity{x_k}_{k=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quantity{x_k}_{k=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout all layers.
Hence the attention matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is layer-invariant, and we drop the layer index, writing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="wK_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">wK_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> realizes the softmax weight <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) with the score function <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(S_{j},S_{k-1})=\langle x(S_{j}),x(S_{k-1})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(S_{j},S_{k-1})=\langle x(S_{j}),x(S_{k-1})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Target-value head.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The target-value head reuses the same parameters <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{l},A_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{l},A_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and first forms the same kernel aggregation as in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).
It then routes the aggregated quantities to the predecessor columns via a shift matrix</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\Pi\doteq\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
1&amp;0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
0&amp;\cdots&amp;1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;\cdots&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\Pi\doteq\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
1&amp;0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
0&amp;\cdots&amp;1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;\cdots&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This head writes an additive update to row <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+2</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle\Delta Z_{l}[d+2,:]=\gamma\,\big(V_{l}Z_{l}\,\mathrm{softmax}(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})\Pi\big)[d+3,:]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\Delta Z_{l}[d+2,:]=\gamma\,\big(V_{l}Z_{l}\,\mathrm{softmax}(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})\Pi\big)[d+3,:].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting layerwise recursion sums the contributions of the two heads as</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle Z_{l+1}\doteq Z_{l}+\Delta Z_{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle Z_{l+1}\doteq Z_{l}+\Delta Z_{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Recall (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we define the scalar output at layer <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle v_{l}(S_{n})\doteq Z_{l}[d+3,n+1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle v_{l}(S_{n})\doteq Z_{l}[d+3,n+1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows that <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{l}(S_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{l}(S_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> evolves according to the weighted softmax TD update.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer dual-head Transformer with recursion (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) and parameters <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quantity{(V_l, A_l)}_{l=0}^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quantity{(V_l, A_l)}_{l=0}^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math> given by (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), initialized at <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).
Then the forward pass updates <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{l}(S_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{l}(S_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle v_{l+1}(S_{n})=v_{l}(S_{n})+\sum_{k=1}^{n}\delta_{k}^{(l)}K(S_{k-1},S_{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle v_{l+1}(S_{n})=v_{l}(S_{n})+\sum_{k=1}^{n}\delta_{k}^{(l)}K(S_{k-1},S_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is in Section <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a></span>.
Since (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) matches the (<a href="#S3.Ex1" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">weighted softmax TD</span></a>) update with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> as the iteration index, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the Transformer forward pass can implement iterations of the (<a href="#S3.Ex1" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">weighted softmax TD</span></a>) update at inference time.
We refer to this implementation paradigm as softmax in-context TD (softmax ICTD).
For simplicity, we set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{l}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{l}=1</annotation></semantics></math> throughout.
The general case follows by replacing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{l}V_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{l}V_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which rescales the update magnitude
but does not alter the structure of the construction in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Dual-Head Attention = Single-Head Attention + Temporal Shift Module</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dual-head block in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is redundant and inconvenient for analysis.
Each layer computes two heads that share the same term <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{l}Z_{l}\mathrm{softmax}(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{l}Z_{l}\mathrm{softmax}(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) applies <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> to the same aggregated quantities in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and scales them by <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> before writing.
Motivated by this observation, we reparameterize the block as a single-head attention followed by a parameter-free temporal shift module (TSM; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2019)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{l},A_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{l},A_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), define</p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle Z_{l+\frac{1}{2}}\doteq Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle Z_{l+\frac{1}{2}}\doteq Z_{l}+V_{l}Z_{l}\mathrm{softmax}\bigl(Z_{l}^{\top}A_{l}Z_{l}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which matches the update in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) on the current-memory row.
In the spirit of TSM, we introduce two fixed shift matrices</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle U\doteq I-e_{d+2}e_{d+2}^{\top},\qquad W\doteq\gamma\,e_{d+2}e_{d+3}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle U\doteq I-e_{d+2}e_{d+2}^{\top},\qquad W\doteq\gamma\,e_{d+2}e_{d+3}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Recall from (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) that <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one-step predecessor shift matrix.
Therefore, <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> clears the target-memory row, and <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(\cdot)\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(\cdot)\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> routes the <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-scaled current-memory row to the predecessor columns of the target-memory row; see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Dual-Head Attention = Single-Head Attention + Temporal Shift Module ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for an illustration.
The resulting single-head+TSM update is</p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle Z_{l+1}=UZ_{l+\frac{1}{2}}+WZ_{l+\frac{1}{2}}\Pi." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle Z_{l+1}=UZ_{l+\frac{1}{2}}+WZ_{l+\frac{1}{2}}\Pi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We next show that (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In 4 Dual-Head Attention = Single-Head Attention + Temporal Shift Module ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is exactly equivalent to the original dual-head attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). Under the parameter correspondence
in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=0,\ldots,L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=0,\ldots,L-1</annotation></semantics></math> the dual-head block (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>)
and the single-head+TSM block (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In 4 Dual-Head Attention = Single-Head Attention + Temporal Shift Module ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) map the same input <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> to the
same output <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is in Section <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix C Proofs in Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a></span>.
In the rest of the paper, we analyze inference-time behavior using the single-head+TSM form.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:89.7pt;"><img src="2605.07333v1/temporal.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/88;" width="124" height="88" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F2.2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) <math id="S4.F2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l+\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l+\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:89.7pt;"><img src="2605.07333v1/shifted.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/88;" width="124" height="88" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F2.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b) <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{l+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (left-shift with <math id="S4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, right pad 0).</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Original vs. shifted memory rows.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Inference-Time Convergence</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now address <a href="#S1.I1.i2" title="item (Q2) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q2)</span></a> by establishing the convergence of softmax ICTD
to the true value function <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of layers <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
and the context length <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout the rest of the paper,
we assume the trajectory <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}=(S_{0},R_{1},\ldots,S_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}=(S_{0},R_{1},\ldots,S_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> visits every state,
i.e., <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{0},S_{1},\ldots,S_{n-1}\}=\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{0},S_{1},\ldots,S_{n-1}\}=\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the recursion (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) on <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, for a given context
trajectory <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S5.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle\widehat{M}_{n}(s,s^{\prime})\doteq\sum_{k=1}^{n}K(S_{k-1},s)\,\mathbbm{1}\{S_{k-1}=s^{\prime}\},\quad\widehat{P}_{n}(s,s^{\prime})\doteq\sum_{k=1}^{n}K(S_{k-1},s)\,\mathbbm{1}\{S_{k}=s^{\prime}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\widehat{M}_{n}(s,s^{\prime})\doteq\sum_{k=1}^{n}K(S_{k-1},s)\,\mathbbm{1}\{S_{k-1}=s^{\prime}\},\quad\widehat{P}_{n}(s,s^{\prime})\doteq\sum_{k=1}^{n}K(S_{k-1},s)\,\mathbbm{1}\{S_{k}=s^{\prime}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{M}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{M}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates the softmax weights according to the predecessor
state <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates the same weights according to
the successor state <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ Target-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) can be written compactly as</p>
<table id="S5.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v)=(I-\widehat{M}_{n}+\gamma\widehat{P}_{n})v+\widehat{M}_{n}r," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v)=(I-\widehat{M}_{n}+\gamma\widehat{P}_{n})v+\widehat{M}_{n}r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and stacking layers corresponds to repeated application of <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle v_{l+1}=\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v_{l})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle v_{l+1}=\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v_{l}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical operator <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> contracts whenever <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{M}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{M}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has
sufficiently large diagonal entries. To formalize this, we introduce the
population counterpart of <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{M}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{M}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained by replacing the empirical
state frequencies in <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with the stationary distribution <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle M_{\pi}(s,s^{\prime})\doteq\frac{\mu_{\pi}(s^{\prime})\,\exp(\langle x(s), x(s') \rangle)}{\sum_{u\in\mathcal{S}}\mu_{\pi}(u)\,\exp(\langle x(s), x(u) \rangle)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle M_{\pi}(s,s^{\prime})\doteq\frac{\mu_{\pi}(s^{\prime})\,\exp(\langle x(s), x(s') \rangle)}{\sum_{u\in\mathcal{S}}\mu_{\pi}(u)\,\exp(\langle x(s), x(u) \rangle)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stationary distribution of <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. We impose the
following contraction assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a constant <math id="S5.Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}}\in\bigl(0,\frac{1-\gamma}{2}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a></mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}}\in\bigl(0,\frac{1-\gamma}{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\min_{s\in\mathcal{S}}M_{\pi}(s,s)\geq\frac{1+\gamma}{2}+C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a></mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\min_{s\in\mathcal{S}}M_{\pi}(s,s)\geq\frac{1+\gamma}{2}+C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> is a kernel diagonality condition on <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, requiring <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\pi}(s,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\pi}(s,s)</annotation></semantics></math> to dominate the off-diagonal mass at every state <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is expected to hold when the score function <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(s,s^{\prime})=\langle x(s),x(s^{\prime})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(s,s^{\prime})=\langle x(s),x(s^{\prime})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> separates self-pairs from cross-pairs, i.e., <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(s,s)-g(s,s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(s,s)-g(s,s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently positive for <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}\neq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}\neq s</annotation></semantics></math>, which sharpens the softmax in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) toward the diagonal.
We will see in Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Experiments ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> that this self-concentration emerges empirically over pretraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough, <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{M}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{M}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> inherits the diagonal margin of <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, which together with a high-probability bound on the bias <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v_{\pi})-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v_{\pi})-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> yields our main convergence result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exist constants <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},1},C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},2},C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},3}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},1},C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},2},C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},3}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
such that if <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},1}\log(1/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},1}\log(1/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\|v_{L}-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}\leq(1-C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}})^{L}\|v_{0}-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}+C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},2}\sqrt{\frac{\log(C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},3}/\delta)}{n}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a></mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:50%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\|v_{L}-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}\leq(1-C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}})^{L}\|v_{0}-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}+C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},2}\sqrt{\frac{\log(C_{\text{Thm}\ref{thm:finite-n},3}/\delta)}{n}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is in Section <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix D Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a>, which combines an
<math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-contraction of <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ D.2 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix D Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) with a high-probability bound on the bias
<math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v_{\pi})-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{\mathcal{T}}_{\widetilde{K}}(v_{\pi})-v_{\pi}\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma6" title="Lemma 6. ‣ D.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix D Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>),
both deferred to Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix D Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix D Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>
respectively. The first term of (<a href="#S5.E29" title="In Theorem 2. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) decays geometrically with
the number of layers <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, while the second term is a statistical floor
that vanishes as <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, the softmax ICTD recursion
converges to <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> in the double limit where both depth and context
length grow.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Emergence of Softmax ICTD under Reinforcement Pretraining</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now answer <a href="#S1.I1.i3" title="item (Q3) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(Q3)</span></a> by showing that, as the depth <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and the context length <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameters <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(V_{l},A_{l})\}_{l=0}^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(V_{l},A_{l})\}_{l=0}^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math> constructed in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) are one of the global minimizers of the reinforcement pretraining loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid specializing to a single task, we pretrain on a distribution of MRPs drawn from a joint distribution <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> over transition probabilities and reward functions, i.e., <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,r)\sim\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,r)\sim\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each sampled MRP, we draw a trajectory <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n+1}=(S_{0},R_{1},S_{1},\ldots,R_{n+1},S_{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n+1}=(S_{0},R_{1},S_{1},\ldots,R_{n+1},S_{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> from the stationary Markov chain induced by <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}\sim\mu_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}\sim\mu_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>).
We construct the prompt <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from the first <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> transitions <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{0},R_{1},\ldots,R_{n},S_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{0},R_{1},\ldots,R_{n},S_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), and denote the scalar output of the <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer Transformer by <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{L}(S_{n};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{L}(S_{n};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{L}(S_{n+1};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{L}(S_{n+1};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the output on the shifted prompt built from <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{n+1},S_{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{n+1},S_{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> with query <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The TD error at the query state is</p>
<table id="S6.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\delta_{L}(\theta)\doteq R_{n+1}+\gamma v_{L}(S_{n+1};\theta)-v_{L}(S_{n};\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\delta_{L}(\theta)\doteq R_{n+1}+\gamma v_{L}(S_{n+1};\theta)-v_{L}(S_{n};\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The norm of the expected update (NEU) is a fundamental objective in TD methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al., 2009</a>)</cite>. Its zeros characterize TD fixed points, making it a natural pretraining loss for studying whether the constructed parameters <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> attain global optimality. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, we adopt its extension to arbitrary function approximation, defined as the <math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of the expected TD update direction:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle J_{L,n}(\theta)\doteq\bigl\|\mathbb{E}_{(p,r),\,\tau_{n+1}}[\delta_{L}(\theta)\nabla_{\theta}v_{L}(S_{n};\theta)]\bigr\|_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≐</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle J_{L,n}(\theta)\doteq\bigl\|\mathbb{E}_{(p,r),\,\tau_{n+1}}[\delta_{L}(\theta)\nabla_{\theta}v_{L}(S_{n};\theta)]\bigr\|_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Following common practice in the in-context learning theory literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Oswald et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahankali et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gatmiry et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, we analyze NEU on a looped Transformer and restrict the parameters to a structured sparse space.
Concretely, we tie the parameters across layers by sharing the same pair <math id="S6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{l},A_{l})=(V,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{l},A_{l})=(V,A)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{0,\dots,L-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{0,\dots,L-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and search over the following parameter space:</p>
<table id="S6.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle\Theta^{\text{Looped}}\doteq\bigg\{\theta=(V,A)^{L}\bigg|V=\begin{bmatrix}0_{(d+2)\times d}&amp;0_{(d+2)\times 3}\\
0_{1\times d}&amp;u\end{bmatrix},A=\begin{bmatrix}B&amp;0_{d\times 3}\\
0_{3\times d}&amp;0_{3\times 3}\end{bmatrix}\bigg\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mtext>Looped</mtext></msup><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo fence="false" maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\Theta^{\text{Looped}}\doteq\bigg\{\theta=(V,A)^{L}\bigg|V=\begin{bmatrix}0_{(d+2)\times d}&amp;0_{(d+2)\times 3}\\
0_{1\times d}&amp;u\end{bmatrix},A=\begin{bmatrix}B&amp;0_{d\times 3}\\
0_{3\times d}&amp;0_{3\times 3}\end{bmatrix}\bigg\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the parameter choice constructed in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) lies in <math id="S6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{\text{Looped}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mtext>Looped</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{\text{Looped}}</annotation></semantics></math>; for convenience, we denote it by <math id="S6.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now state the main emergence result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> holding uniformly over <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exist a constant <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and constants
<math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},2},C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},3}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 6 Emergence of Softmax ICTD under Reinforcement Pretraining ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 6 Emergence of Softmax ICTD under Reinforcement Pretraining ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},2},C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},3}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for every <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle J_{L,n}(\theta_{*})\leq C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},2}\quantity[
(1-C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}})^L
+
\sqrt{\frac{\log n}{n}}
]+C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},3}\frac{L(2+\gamma)^{2L}}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 6 Emergence of Softmax ICTD under Reinforcement Pretraining ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a></mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Thm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 6 Emergence of Softmax ICTD under Reinforcement Pretraining ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle J_{L,n}(\theta_{*})\leq C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},2}\quantity[
(1-C_{\ref{ass:diagonal-margin}})^L
+
\sqrt{\frac{\log n}{n}}
]+C_{\text{Thm}\text{\ref{thm:emergence}},3}\frac{L(2+\gamma)^{2L}}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\lim_{L\to\infty}\limsup_{n\to\infty}J_{L,n}(\theta_{*})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\lim_{L\to\infty}\limsup_{n\to\infty}J_{L,n}(\theta_{*})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is in Section <a href="#A5.SS4" title="E.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix E Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>. The argument relies on a uniform bound on <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}v_{L}(S_{n};\theta_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}v_{L}(S_{n};\theta_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> across all depths (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma10" title="Lemma 10. ‣ E.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix E Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix E Proofs of Section ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>), which in turn uses Assumption <a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> together with the layer-invariance of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\Theta^{\text{Looped}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mtext>Looped</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\Theta^{\text{Looped}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{L,n}(\theta)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{L,n}(\theta)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 6 Emergence of Softmax ICTD under Reinforcement Pretraining ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
implies that <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a global minimizer of the NEU loss in the iterated regime where the context length grows first and the depth grows afterward.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical study of ICRL has largely relied on the linear-attention abstraction, which replaces the softmax with the identity map to make the forward pass tractable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
This simplification is also pervasive in the broader in-context learning (ICL) literature, where it has been used to show that Transformers can implement gradient-based algorithms in their forward pass <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gatmiry et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
A smaller line of work keeps a nonlinearity explicit, either through general activation operators <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VZ\,h(Z^{\top}AZ)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VZ\,h(Z^{\top}AZ)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or by relating softmax attention to kernel-based learning rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren and Liu, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dragutinović et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
While these results inform supervised in-context computation, they do not address reinforcement-pretrained policy evaluation, and the standard softmax used in practical Transformers remains largely unanalyzed in the ICRL setting.
Our work closes this gap, complementing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> by removing the linear-attention simplification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A concurrent submission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2026</a>)</cite> similarly builds on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, but takes a fundamentally different route: it analyzes linear attention with autoregressive chain-of-thought generation and linear function approximation, where each generated token implements one batch TD update on the context,
whereas we analyze softmax attention with depth-based computation for finite-state policy evaluation.
As a result, the two works draw on largely disjoint technical tools.
For convergence, our analysis is driven by a kernel diagonality condition (Assumption <a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) and is then lifted to finite contexts via concentration, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2026)</a></cite> provide a finite-sample analysis on a single Markovian trajectory.
For emergence, we control how the value prediction depends on the parameters, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2026)</a></cite> control how the algorithmic iterates depend on the parameters.
The two works share the Boyan’s chain task setup of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">ICRL has been studied under both supervised and reinforcement pretraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moeini et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Supervised pretraining trains a sequence model to imitate trajectories from existing RL algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laskin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu and Abbeel, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirsch et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zisman et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zisman et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Polubarov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
with theoretical accounts of the resulting in-context behavior
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Reinforcement pretraining instead trains the model with an RL objective and observes that test-time adaptation still emerges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ritter et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stadie et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zintgraf et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Melo, 2022</a>)</cite>, with empirical demonstrations across diverse tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bauer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grigsby et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grigsby et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elawady et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cook et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Theoretical results under reinforcement pretraining remain limited: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2025)</a></cite> analyze a regret-minimization objective, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> establish convergence and emergence under linear attention. Our paper falls in this regime, with softmax attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, we study multi-task policy evaluation on randomized instances of Boyan’s chain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boyan, 1999</a>)</cite>.
At the beginning of each epoch, we sample a Boyan-chain MRP together with a feature map (Algorithm <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Task distribution. ‣ F.3 Boyan’s chain tasks and experiment setup ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Details ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span>).
We construct the prompt <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Weighted softmax TD. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and apply the Transformer block in Section <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"><a href="#S4" title="4 Dual-Head Attention = Single-Head Attention + Temporal Shift Module ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></span>,
using a column-normalized softmax attention kernel as in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, the block unrolls autoregressively for <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math> layers with shared parameters <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{l},A_{l})=(V_{0},A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{l},A_{l})=(V_{0},A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> across layers.
All entries of <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable except for a minimal sparse parameterization that prevents overwriting features or rewards; see Section <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 Minimal Sparse Parameterization ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Details ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>.
Hyperparameter and task-generation details are deferred to Section <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"><a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Boyan’s chain tasks and experiment setup ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Details ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S8.F3.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:148.3pt;"><img src="2605.07333v1/best_VA_emergence_mean_V0_A0.png" id="S8.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:332/165;" width="332" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S8.F3.2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S8.F3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Learned <math id="S8.F3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S8.F3.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:182.9pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07333v1/emerVSdiag0p0.svg" id="S8.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:246/165;" width="246" height="165"></object>
<p id="S8.F3.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S8.F3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b) <math id="S8.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> versus kernel diagonal mean <math id="S8.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
Emergence of the learned TD block.
(a) Learned <math id="S8.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at the checkpoint maximizing
<math id="S8.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(V_{\mathrm{em}},A_{\mathrm{em}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(V_{\mathrm{em}},A_{\mathrm{em}})</annotation></semantics></math>, averaged over <math id="S8.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> seeds.
Each matrix is rescaled by its maximum absolute entry for visualization.
(b) Evolution of <math id="S8.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the kernel diagonal mean <math id="S8.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We record <math id="S8.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{t},V_{\mathrm{em}},A_{\mathrm{em}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{t},V_{\mathrm{em}},A_{\mathrm{em}})</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S8.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3000</annotation></semantics></math> sampled MRPs and aggregate over <math id="S8.F3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> seeds;
shaded regions indicate variability across seeds.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TD emergence.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We quantify emergence using two scores <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{em}},A_{\mathrm{em}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{em}},A_{\mathrm{em}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> that measure the similarity between the learned first-layer parameters <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and the ideal TD block in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>); see Section <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Emergence metrics ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Details ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> for definitions.
Figure <a href="#S8.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Setup. ‣ 8 Experiments ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a) visualizes <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> at the checkpoint maximizing <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(V_{\rm em},A_{\rm em})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(V_{\rm em},A_{\rm em})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3000</annotation></semantics></math> sampled MRPs.
The bottom-right entries of <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibit the <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+,+,-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+,+,-)</annotation></semantics></math> sign pattern on <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,\gamma v^{\prime},v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,\gamma v^{\prime},v)</annotation></semantics></math>, closely matching (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Current-value head. ‣ 3 Transformers Can Implement In-Context Weighted Softmax TD ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).
Meanwhile, the upper left <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> blocks of <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes sharply diagonal, providing a second signature of emergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Kernel diagonality.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we relate emergence to Assumption <a href="#S5.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 5.1. ‣ 5 Inference-Time Convergence ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.
At training step <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{(t)}\doteq Z_{0}^{\top}A_{0}^{(t)}Z_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≐</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{(t)}\doteq Z_{0}^{\top}A_{0}^{(t)}Z_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{K}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{K}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the attention matrix
in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Transformers and Softmax Self-Attention ‣ 2 Background ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) with <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=A_{0}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=A_{0}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We summarize its kernel diagonality by the kernel diagonal mean</p>
<table id="S8.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle d_{t}\doteq\frac{1}{n}\tr(\wK^{(t)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle d_{t}\doteq\frac{1}{n}\tr(\wK^{(t)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S8.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Setup. ‣ 8 Experiments ‣ Beyond Linear Attention: Softmax Transformers Implement In-Context Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) plots <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We find that <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains high across a wide range of <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{em}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>em</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{em}}</annotation></semantics></math> starts to rise only after <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> reaches an intermediate level.
This suggests that learning the TD update direction in the bottom-right entries of <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> requires a sufficient kernel diagonality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the first theoretical analysis of in-context policy evaluation beyond the linear-attention simplification, under softmax attention.
We show by construction that a Transformer layer can be configured to implement a weighted softmax TD update step in its forward pass.
We prove an inference-time convergence guarantee as the number of layers increases under a suitable contraction condition, and show that the desired parameters are one of the global minimizers of a pretraining loss, explaining their emergence with increasingly diagonal attention in our experiments.
Future work includes relaxing the contraction requirement and extending the theory and experiments to sampled trajectories and control settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
